EXPERIMENT 29 – PATH COVERAGE TESTING USING JUNIT
AIM
To write JUnit test cases to execute path coverage testing on a payroll calculation program using JUnit.
ALGORITHM
1. Create a Maven project and add the JUnit 5 junit-jupiter dependency to pom.xml.
2. Create the Payroll class with the netSalary(basic, overtimeHours) method.
3. Include three decisions: overtime greater than 0, gross salary greater than 50,000 and gross salary greater than 25,000.
4. Calculate cyclomatic complexity:
   
   \[
   V(G) = \text{Number of decisions} + 1 = 3+1=4
   \]
5. Identify four independent paths:
   - P1: No overtime, no tax.
   - P2: Overtime, no tax.
   - P3: 10% tax.
   - P4: 20% tax.
6. Write one JUnit test for each path using assertEquals() with a delta value.
7. Run tests using mvn test and verify all four paths.
CODE
Payroll.java
public class Payroll {
    public static double netSalary(double basic, int overtimeHours) {
        double gross = basic;

        if (overtimeHours > 0) {
            gross += overtimeHours * 200;
        }

        double tax;

        if (gross > 50000) {
            tax = gross * 0.20;
        } else if (gross > 25000) {
            tax = gross * 0.10;
        } else {
            tax = 0;
        }

        return gross - tax;
    }
}


PayrollPathTest.java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class PayrollPathTest {

    private static final double DELTA = 0.01;

    @Test
    void path1_noOvertime_noTax() {
        assertEquals(20000, Payroll.netSalary(20000, 0), DELTA);
    }

    @Test
    void path2_overtime_noTax() {
        assertEquals(22000, Payroll.netSalary(20000, 10), DELTA);
    }

    @Test
    void path3_tenPercentTax() {
        assertEquals(27000, Payroll.netSalary(30000, 0), DELTA);
    }

    @Test
    void path4_twentyPercentTax() {
        assertEquals(48000, Payroll.netSalary(60000, 0), DELTA);
    }
}


